{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion bias: The model consistently forecasts a slow, monotonic drift (up or down) while true prices exhibit sharp, high-amplitude swings. Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast slope to account for sudden spikes and crashes not captured by the trend.",
    "Invert sentiment signals for short-term horizons: Positive sentiment (1.0) often precedes immediate price drops or stagnation in this dataset, while negative sentiment (-1.0) frequently coincides with price rallies. Treat strong sentiment spikes as contrarian indicators for the first 5-10 days.",
    "Reduce forecast lag on reversals: The model fails to react to the initial 2-3 days of a trend reversal (e.g., the drop from 76 to 60 in Jan 2024). Accelerate the forecast adjustment rate when the 3-day rolling momentum diverges significantly from the model's projected path.",
    "Apply overshoot dampening after spikes: Following a price spike (e.g., the rise to 76.67 in May 2024), the model underestimates the subsequent decline. Force a steeper downward correction in the forecast for days 5-15 post-spike to match the observed rapid mean reversion.",
    "Discard long-term monotonic projections: The 30-day forecast horizon is unreliable for predicting direction. Replace the tail of the forecast (days 15-30) with a flat mean-reversion line anchored to the most recent 5-day average rather than the model's extrapolated trend."
  ]
}
